{"id":"7c184764-38ca-45ba-8a92-4ea54c40bcc4","name":"MCP turns intent into action","path":"WWW / MCP Journal / MCP turns intent into action","docTypeId":"1c74e098-31f2-478f-9c13-75b3e17a1cf7","templateId":"eec62926-28d6-4564-bdda-d6621700033e","state":"Published","domains":[],"values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextArea","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"excerpt","value":"MCP gives AI assistants a consistent way to discover tools and use them with the context they need. That simple bridge turns a request into a useful action."},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.BlockGrid","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"blocks","value":{"layout":{"Umbraco.BlockGrid":[{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"3266ed1e-df70-4f7a-bf2e-39e8f6e7ff1b","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"d9e2a40e-9937-4d85-bf74-198c019df746","settingsKey":null}]},"contentData":[{"contentTypeKey":"25ac84b8-9614-4204-9f33-72f501b77d5c","key":"3266ed1e-df70-4f7a-bf2e-39e8f6e7ff1b","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextBox","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"heading","value":"MCP turns intent into action"},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextArea","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"intro","value":"A calmer way for people and software to work together."},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.MediaPicker3","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"image","value":[{"key":"5448ea74-74f6-4733-9442-bf8e55f212ca","mediaKey":"78ca2de3-61c1-4eba-be24-81524839d589","mediaTypeAlias":"Image","crops":[],"focalPoint":null}]}]},{"contentTypeKey":"85e5beaa-2ae4-4b16-a687-aa22ca6a3ae9","key":"d9e2a40e-9937-4d85-bf74-198c019df746","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"body","value":{"markup":"<p>Software gets more useful when it can reach the tools where work already happens. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) gives AI applications a common way to connect to those tools and the data around them.</p><h2>One connection, many capabilities</h2><p>Without a shared protocol, every assistant and service needs its own custom integration. MCP gives servers a predictable way to describe capabilities, so a client can discover what is available and call the right tool when a task needs it.</p><p>That means an assistant can move from explaining how to do something to actually doing it: look up a record, make a change, or check the result. The user can stay focused on the outcome while the tool handles the details.</p><h2>Context makes the difference</h2><p>A tool call is most helpful when it is grounded in the right information. MCP can expose resources and prompts alongside tools, giving the client a clearer picture of the system it is working with.</p><p>The result is a more natural loop: understand the request, inspect available context, take a bounded action, and report what happened. That is why MCP feels less like another chat feature and more like a practical interface to software.</p><p><em>Photo: Chris Ried / Unsplash.</em></p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"expose":[]}}}]}],"settingsData":[],"expose":[{"contentKey":"3266ed1e-df70-4f7a-bf2e-39e8f6e7ff1b","culture":null,"segment":null},{"contentKey":"d9e2a40e-9937-4d85-bf74-198c019df746","culture":null,"segment":null}]}}],"urls":["/mcp-turns-intent-into-action/"],"docType":"blogPostPage","template":"blogPost"} 

{"id":"46cde6c1-d537-4d1d-b927-e5e04a6b6261","name":"One context for every tool","path":"WWW / MCP Journal / One context for every tool","docTypeId":"1c74e098-31f2-478f-9c13-75b3e17a1cf7","templateId":"eec62926-28d6-4564-bdda-d6621700033e","state":"Published","domains":[],"values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextArea","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"excerpt","value":"MCP makes integrations easier to reuse. A consistent interface lets teams add new tools without rebuilding the entire assistant around each service."},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.BlockGrid","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"blocks","value":{"layout":{"Umbraco.BlockGrid":[{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"d50a90d6-5bc9-40f1-91eb-e3c911ef6f88","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"fea71684-a141-4a42-9222-95e8b682c6ba","settingsKey":null}]},"contentData":[{"contentTypeKey":"25ac84b8-9614-4204-9f33-72f501b77d5c","key":"d50a90d6-5bc9-40f1-91eb-e3c911ef6f88","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextBox","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"heading","value":"One context for every tool"},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextArea","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"intro","value":"Open connections make capable assistants easier to extend."},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.MediaPicker3","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"image","value":[{"key":"25e3a0bb-337a-43e1-8eaa-b5b8f40d639a","mediaKey":"098864ff-b3a5-4d18-8ad1-ef5659078af1","mediaTypeAlias":"Image","crops":[],"focalPoint":null}]}]},{"contentTypeKey":"85e5beaa-2ae4-4b16-a687-aa22ca6a3ae9","key":"fea71684-a141-4a42-9222-95e8b682c6ba","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"body","value":{"markup":"<p>The promise of MCP is not only that an assistant can call tools. It is that those connections can be added in a consistent way, across different clients and different systems.</p><h2>Build the connection once</h2><p>A team can expose a useful capability through an MCP server, then make it available to compatible clients. The server describes the tools it offers and the inputs they expect. Clients can inspect that description and decide which capability fits the user's request.</p><p>This gives product teams room to specialize. A content system can expose content operations; a database can expose safe queries; a local development environment can expose project tools. Each one can focus on its own domain while the assistant works across them.</p><h2>Keep people in the loop</h2><p>Clear tool names and structured inputs make it easier to understand what an assistant is about to do. Systems can keep permissions and approval steps close to the action, while the client presents the result in the same conversation.</p><p>That combination—reusable connections, discoverable capabilities, and a clear record of the action—makes MCP an appealing foundation for useful automation. It lets the ecosystem grow one well-defined tool at a time.</p><p><em>Photo: Vardan Papikyan / Unsplash.</em></p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"expose":[]}}}]}],"settingsData":[],"expose":[{"contentKey":"d50a90d6-5bc9-40f1-91eb-e3c911ef6f88","culture":null,"segment":null},{"contentKey":"fea71684-a141-4a42-9222-95e8b682c6ba","culture":null,"segment":null}]}}],"urls":["/one-context-for-every-tool/"],"docType":"blogPostPage","template":"blogPost"} 

